    "summary": "Gainesville's ramps were different styles than any prior split, with many wide intersections. A few new or renovated intersections carry large diagonal curb ramps spanning both crossing directions - a design not seen in earlier cities. The model's false negatives clustered on two apparent causes: significant debris sitting on the ramp surface, and distance. Reviewer-rated confidence is HIGH: the rubric fit and the judgment calls were minor.",
    "caveats": [
      "Novel ramp styles. Different ramp designs than previous splits, including large diagonal curb ramps at new/renovated intersections that stretch across both crossing directions. Combined with many wide intersections, this is the split's distinctive fabric; it did not fight the rubric.",
      "Debris. Some false negatives appeared to be caused by significant debris on the ramp - a surface-legibility failure rather than a geometry one. Reviewer impression, not a measurement.",
      "Distant ramps. Other misses were far-field, echoing the annapolis and paterson distance findings. Impression - the Phase-4 distance analysis should quantify it."
    ]
  },
